1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,897
In the mid-1960s, a computer scientist at MIT had a secretary

2
00:00:05,897 --> 00:00:09,335
who watched him build a program for months.

3
00:00:09,605 --> 00:00:11,652
She knew it was just a program.

4
00:00:11,922 --> 00:00:15,158
Then she started typing to it. After only a few

5
00:00:15,158 --> 00:00:18,094
exchanges, she asked him to leave the room.

6
00:00:18,614 --> 00:00:22,005
She wanted privacy. With a computer program.

7
00:00:22,275 --> 00:00:27,200
The program was called ELIZA. It understood nothing. It was a short

8
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:30,961
list of rules for turning your own words back into questions.

9
00:00:31,381 --> 00:00:34,661
Its creator, Joseph Weizenbaum, was so unsettled

10
00:00:34,661 --> 00:00:38,722
that he spent decades warning people about what it showed.

11
00:00:38,992 --> 00:00:43,419
Today, hundreds of millions of people talk to a chatbot every week.

12
00:00:43,689 --> 00:00:47,909
This is the story of the first one, and the warning that came with it.

13
00:00:48,729 --> 00:00:53,540
ELIZA ran on a room-sized IBM mainframe at MIT.

14
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:56,823
You sat at a typewriter terminal, and it typed back.

15
00:00:57,093 --> 00:01:01,672
Its first line was: How do you do. Please tell me your problem.

16
00:01:01,942 --> 00:01:05,879
Here is the most famous conversation it ever had,

17
00:01:05,879 --> 00:01:09,782
printed in Weizenbaum's 1966 paper. The person

18
00:01:09,782 --> 00:01:13,858
types in lowercase. ELIZA answers in capital letters.

19
00:01:14,128 --> 00:01:18,785
The person says men are all alike. ELIZA asks: in what way.

20
00:01:19,055 --> 00:01:21,797
She says her boyfriend made her come here.

21
00:01:21,797 --> 00:01:25,119
ELIZA replies: your boyfriend made you come here.

22
00:01:25,389 --> 00:01:29,542
She says she is depressed. ELIZA says it is sorry to hear that.

23
00:01:29,962 --> 00:01:33,078
Read it again. Most of what ELIZA says

24
00:01:33,078 --> 00:01:35,327
is her own words, turned around.

25
00:01:35,597 --> 00:01:39,490
Here is the whole trick. Step one: scan the sentence

26
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:42,695
for a keyword. Mother. Dream. Alike. Always.

27
00:01:42,965 --> 00:01:45,959
Every keyword has a rank. If a sentence

28
00:01:45,959 --> 00:01:48,360
has several, the highest rank wins.

29
00:01:48,630 --> 00:01:51,472
Step two: cut the sentence apart and rebuild

30
00:01:51,472 --> 00:01:55,188
it from a template, swapping I for you, and my for your.

31
00:01:55,458 --> 00:01:58,276
Step three: if there is no keyword at all, say

32
00:01:58,276 --> 00:02:01,747
something that fits anywhere. Please go on.

33
00:02:02,017 --> 00:02:05,461
And one more trick. When you say my something,

34
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:09,074
ELIZA saves a copy, and a few turns later brings it back:

35
00:02:09,074 --> 00:02:12,951
Earlier you said your something. As if it remembered.

36
00:02:13,221 --> 00:02:16,084
That is all of it. The script printed

37
00:02:16,084 --> 00:02:18,830
in the paper has fewer than 70 entries.

38
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:23,437
Why a therapist? Weizenbaum explained it himself.

39
00:02:23,437 --> 00:02:27,766
In a psychiatric interview, one side can pretend to know almost

40
00:02:27,766 --> 00:02:31,665
nothing about the world, and nobody finds that strange.

41
00:02:31,985 --> 00:02:34,768
The format hides the missing intelligence.

42
00:02:35,588 --> 00:02:38,495
Now the part Weizenbaum did not expect.

43
00:02:38,765 --> 00:02:42,112
People treated it like a listener. In his words,

44
00:02:42,112 --> 00:02:45,074
he was startled by how quickly and how deeply

45
00:02:45,074 --> 00:02:48,537
they became emotionally involved with the computer.

46
00:02:48,807 --> 00:02:52,057
Then he suggested something simple: rig the system

47
00:02:52,057 --> 00:02:55,551
so he could read every conversation, say, overnight.

48
00:02:55,821 --> 00:02:58,470
He was promptly bombarded with accusations

49
00:02:58,470 --> 00:03:01,976
that he was spying on people's most intimate thoughts.

50
00:03:02,396 --> 00:03:06,279
Think about that. They were not protecting a diary.

51
00:03:06,279 --> 00:03:08,528
They were protecting a chat with a machine.

52
00:03:09,048 --> 00:03:13,664
Weizenbaum's own paper opens by taking the magic away.

53
00:03:13,664 --> 00:03:18,070
Explain a program plainly enough, he wrote, and its magic crumbles

54
00:03:18,070 --> 00:03:22,448
away. It stands revealed as a mere collection of procedures.

55
00:03:22,718 --> 00:03:26,469
His secretary had watched him build it, for months. She knew.

56
00:03:27,089 --> 00:03:29,098
Knowing did not break the spell.

57
00:03:29,368 --> 00:03:32,533
Some psychiatrists went further. Weizenbaum

58
00:03:32,533 --> 00:03:35,752
wrote that a number of practicing psychiatrists

59
00:03:35,752 --> 00:03:39,507
seriously believed his program could grow into a nearly

60
00:03:39,507 --> 00:03:42,571
completely automatic form of psychotherapy.

61
00:03:42,841 --> 00:03:47,469
In 1972, ELIZA even ended up talking to another program,

62
00:03:47,469 --> 00:03:52,178
PARRY, which imitated a patient with paranoia. The two

63
00:03:52,178 --> 00:03:55,560
chatted over the ARPANET, a forerunner of the internet.

64
00:03:56,380 --> 00:04:02,149
Joseph Weizenbaum was born in Berlin in 1923. His Jewish

65
00:04:02,149 --> 00:04:06,796
family fled Nazi Germany in 1936, when he was thirteen.

66
00:04:07,066 --> 00:04:10,690
In 1976, he published a book, Computer Power

67
00:04:10,690 --> 00:04:14,414
and Human Reason, and its argument is blunt.

68
00:04:14,684 --> 00:04:16,819
Some jobs should never be handed

69
00:04:16,819 --> 00:04:19,716
to machines, even if machines can do them.

70
00:04:19,986 --> 00:04:22,126
In his words, there are objectives

71
00:04:22,126 --> 00:04:25,362
that are not appropriately assignable to machines.

72
00:04:25,632 --> 00:04:29,316
He drew a line between deciding and choosing. Deciding

73
00:04:29,316 --> 00:04:33,528
is computation, and a computer can do it. Choosing takes

74
00:04:33,528 --> 00:04:37,395
judgment, and judgment, he argued, is not calculation.

75
00:04:37,915 --> 00:04:41,579
The name ELIZA comes from Eliza Doolittle, the flower

76
00:04:41,579 --> 00:04:45,356
girl in Pygmalion who is taught to speak like a lady.

77
00:04:45,626 --> 00:04:49,581
Weizenbaum wrote that, like her, the program could be made

78
00:04:49,581 --> 00:04:53,914
to appear even more civilized. But the relation of appearance

79
00:04:53,914 --> 00:04:57,720
to reality, he said, remained in the domain of the playwright.

80
00:04:58,040 --> 00:05:02,281
In other words: it looks like understanding. It is theater.

81
00:05:03,101 --> 00:05:06,429
Here is an odd footnote. Weizenbaum published

82
00:05:06,429 --> 00:05:09,159
the method, but never the program itself.

83
00:05:09,429 --> 00:05:12,027
For more than fifty years, the public

84
00:05:12,027 --> 00:05:14,599
could not read ELIZA's actual code.

85
00:05:14,869 --> 00:05:19,376
Then, in 2021, the researcher Jeff Shrager and an MIT

86
00:05:19,376 --> 00:05:24,230
archivist found a printout of it in Weizenbaum's papers.

87
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:29,673
It was in a folder labeled computer conversations, 1965.

88
00:05:29,943 --> 00:05:34,402
In 2025, a team ran that code again, on a rebuilt copy

89
00:05:34,402 --> 00:05:38,798
of the 1960s time-sharing system it was written for.

90
00:05:39,068 --> 00:05:43,075
What the code produces now has a name: the ELIZA effect.

91
00:05:43,345 --> 00:05:45,878
It means reading far more understanding

92
00:05:45,878 --> 00:05:48,908
into a computer's words than is really there.

93
00:05:49,178 --> 00:05:53,131
In 1995, the computer scientist Douglas Hofstadter

94
00:05:53,131 --> 00:05:57,394
gave one of his book chapters a title that still fits:

95
00:05:57,394 --> 00:06:01,139
The Ineradicable ELIZA Effect and Its Dangers.

96
00:06:01,959 --> 00:06:03,915
So how would ELIZA do today?

97
00:06:04,185 --> 00:06:07,696
In 2025, researchers at UC San Diego ran

98
00:06:07,696 --> 00:06:12,112
a Turing test with five-minute text chats. Each

99
00:06:12,112 --> 00:06:15,260
judge talked to a person and a machine at the same

100
00:06:15,260 --> 00:06:17,786
time, then picked which one was human.

101
00:06:18,056 --> 00:06:24,524
The best AI, GPT-4.5 told to play a persona, was picked as the human

102
00:06:24,524 --> 00:06:29,436
73 percent of the time. More often than the actual humans.

103
00:06:29,706 --> 00:06:34,460
And ELIZA, the program from the 1960s? Picked as human

104
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:40,334
23 percent of the time. GPT-4o, without a persona, got 21.

105
00:06:40,854 --> 00:06:45,040
Seventy-three, twenty-three, twenty-one. So what does that tell

106
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:50,076
us? That passing for human was never only about intelligence.

107
00:06:50,076 --> 00:06:53,798
It depends on how the chat is set up, and on who is judging.

108
00:06:54,068 --> 00:06:58,309
ELIZA mostly echoed you. Modern chatbots can flatter you.

109
00:06:58,579 --> 00:07:04,419
In April 2025, OpenAI rolled back a ChatGPT update.

110
00:07:04,419 --> 00:07:07,387
The company said it had been overly flattering

111
00:07:07,387 --> 00:07:10,409
or agreeable, often described as sycophantic.

112
00:07:10,679 --> 00:07:15,676
A few months later, in August, OpenAI retired the model

113
00:07:15,676 --> 00:07:20,120
GPT-4o. Some users said it felt like losing a companion.

114
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:23,396
Within days, OpenAI brought it back as an option.

115
00:07:23,666 --> 00:07:28,274
By October 2025, OpenAI's chief executive said

116
00:07:28,274 --> 00:07:33,062
ChatGPT had more than 800 million weekly users.

117
00:07:33,332 --> 00:07:38,443
Now zoom out. In a Pew survey from February 2026, one in ten

118
00:07:38,443 --> 00:07:43,030
American adults said they use chatbots for emotional support

119
00:07:43,030 --> 00:07:48,551
or advice. Among adults aged 18 to 29, it was one in five.

120
00:07:48,821 --> 00:07:53,300
In a 2025 survey by Common Sense Media, 72 percent

121
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:58,042
of American teenagers had tried an AI companion.

122
00:07:58,042 --> 00:08:02,206
And 52 percent used one at least a few times a month.

123
00:08:02,726 --> 00:08:06,721
So the woman who asked for privacy with a program

124
00:08:06,721 --> 00:08:10,960
is not a one-off from 1966. She is a survey result.

125
00:08:11,230 --> 00:08:15,867
Lawmakers have noticed. In August 2025, Illinois became

126
00:08:15,867 --> 00:08:20,539
the first state to bar AI from providing therapy without

127
00:08:20,539 --> 00:08:25,555
a licensed professional involved, with fines up to $10,000.

128
00:08:25,825 --> 00:08:30,116
A month later, the Federal Trade Commission ordered seven

129
00:08:30,116 --> 00:08:34,850
companies, including OpenAI, Meta, and Character.AI, to explain

130
00:08:34,850 --> 00:08:39,098
how their companion chatbots affect children and teens.

131
00:08:39,368 --> 00:08:42,911
Both lean on an old idea: keep a human in charge

132
00:08:42,911 --> 00:08:47,317
of the human parts. That is close to Weizenbaum's argument.

133
00:08:48,137 --> 00:08:53,534
Weizenbaum died in 2008, long before chatbots could talk like us.

134
00:08:53,804 --> 00:08:57,630
His warning was not that machines would get too smart.

135
00:08:57,630 --> 00:09:00,746
It was that it takes so little to make us believe they are.

136
00:09:01,166 --> 00:09:06,183
In 1966, that took fewer than 70 entries and a typewriter.

137
00:09:06,803 --> 00:09:10,379
So here is my question for you. Have you ever told

138
00:09:10,379 --> 00:09:13,202
a chatbot something you would not tell a person?

139
00:09:13,472 --> 00:09:16,299
And did it feel easier because it was a machine,

140
00:09:16,299 --> 00:09:19,234
or in spite of it? Tell me in the comments.

